 > Given that potions dilute when you fall into
 > water, I think it fair to say that whatever
 > container these things are in, it isn't
 > water-tight, let alone air-tight.  There may be
 > plenty of headroom for air to be forced out of the
 > container during a slow freeze, allowing the
 > potion to expand while freezing without putting
 > too much pressure on the container.

But that isn't how "containers broken by water
freezing in them" works, even in Real Life. If you
put a straight sided open topped glass tumbler of
water in the freezer, it _will_ break. The water
freezes from outside to inside, and once "out" ice
seals the "in" water away from the surface, its
pressure of expansion is exerted in every direction
equally (not just "up"), breaking the container.

 > This is a lot different than the instantaneous
 > freezing caused by cold magics.

Probably so.

